2.1.8 Chamber Assembly
The assembly of regenerative chambers is unique for each specific chamber design; in short, no two chamber designs are assembled the same. One concept of tubular construction may demand that the coolant tubes be brazed while pressed against a support shell, whereas for others the shell is attached after brazing the tube bundle. However, common problems have arisen during the assembly of most of the combustion chambers. These problems have involved inadvertent tooling damage to thin-wall structures, weld distortion of coolant passages, weld cracking of thin walls, and improper fit of matched parts.
